/*  SEQ 193 -- WHAT THE TWO DRIVES ARE FOR.

    The optional cannot be read out of the generated body: `two()` is spelled
    the same whether two is optional or mandatory, because the modifier lives
    in two's own rStuff min/max and the call site never mentions it. So the
    `?` is asserted behaviourally, by a PAIR:

        optT("a")   optional ABSENT   wins, and `two` never fires
        optT("ab")  optional PRESENT  wins, `two` fires, both chars eaten

    The first row is the one that reads the `?` -- a min left at 1 would make
    optT("a") FAIL. The second is its anti-vacuity sibling -- a chain that
    dropped `two` altogether would pass the first and leave `b` uneaten.

    The body, verbatim on this binary:

        optT = CodE {
            return one() && two();
            }

    and dumpContents reads `one=a string` / `two=b string`, so the labels ARE
    the terms and they carry their own text. The bare-literal spelling did not:
    `"a"-` steals `GrouP` as its tag, both literals emitted `GrouP()`, and
    neither carried its value. The modifier goes on the LABEL -- `one="a"-`
    refuses, `one-="a"` and `two?-="b"` do not.

    `incant/pop/modSeamT` is the fleet copy and pins all four cells (both
    drives, both roads). It stays red on MS-5 only.  */

